## Access Model Comparison

| Tool | Who grants access | Consent required from end user? | Typical use |
|---|---|---|---|
| RDP | Group membership (Remote Desktop Users) | No | Backend admin work, no need for the user to be present |
| Remote Registry | Explicit `winreg` permission + firewall rule | No | Low-level configuration/inventory, rarely used due to lateral-movement risk |
| Remote Assistance | Invitation generated by the user | Yes, explicitly | Walking a user through a fix live, user stays in control throughout |

---

## Key Lessons Learned

- **"Remote access" isn't one permission system.** RDP, Remote Registry, and file/printer sharing are each gated by their own distinct mechanism, and none of them imply the others. Being able to RDP into a machine doesn't mean Remote Registry will also work, since that depends on separate `winreg` permissions and firewall rules entirely.
- **Firewall rules for File and Printer Sharing are disabled by default** on a fresh domain-joined machine, and this silently blocks Remote Registry even when the `winreg` permission itself is correctly configured which is easy to misdiagnose as a permissions problem when it's actually a firewall one.
- **Remote Registry is worth knowing how to use, and also worth knowing why it's often restricted.** It's flagged in hardening guides as a lateral-movement and reconnaissance risk, which is why many real environments disable it entirely except on specific, tightly scoped machines.
- **Consent-based access (Remote Assistance) and administrative access (RDP, Remote Registry) solve different problems.** Remote Assistance is the right tool when the goal is for the user to see and understand the fix, not just have it applied.
